<DocPage
	eyebrow="Install"
	title="Get HippOS running"
	description="Flash the image to a USB drive or microSD card, boot it, and play. Works on PCs and x86 handhelds, the Raspberry Pi 4 and 5, and RK3326 handhelds such as the R36S. On a PC you can install to an internal disk from the menu once you're happy with it."
>
	<h2>What you need</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>One of:
			<ul>
				<li>a 64-bit x86 PC or PC handheld (Steam Deck, ROG Ally, Legion Go, AYANEO…) that boots in UEFI mode and meets the <a href="/hardware#minimum-specs">minimum specs</a>;</li>
				<li>a <a href="/hardware#raspberry-pi">Raspberry Pi 4 or 5</a>;</li>
				<li>an RK3326 handheld (experimental): R36S and its clones, Anbernic RG351M/RG351V, Odroid Go 2/3/Super, Powkiddy RGB10, GameForce Chi, MagicX XU10 and others.</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Something to write it to:
			<ul>
				<li>PC or PC handheld: a USB drive, external SSD or microSD card of <strong>32 GB or more</strong>. About 7 GB to download, 12 GB once written.</li>
				<li>Raspberry Pi: a microSD card or USB drive of <strong>32 GB or more</strong>. About 5 GB to download, 11 GB once written.</li>
				<li>RK3326 handheld: a microSD card of <strong>16 GB or more</strong>. About 4 GB to download, 10 GB once written. Keep the handheld's original card: the R36S needs a file from it.</li>
			</ul>
		</li>
		<li>Membership of the HippOS Discord with the Early Access role. HippOS is in early access, and downloads are linked to Discord.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>1. Download</h2>
	<p>
		Sign in with Discord on the <a href="/download">download page</a> and get the image for your hardware: PCs and x86
		handhelds, Raspberry Pi 4 and 5, or RK3326 handhelds. The links always point at the latest beta.
	</p>

	<h2>2. Write it to a drive</h2>
	<p>Writing the image erases everything on the target drive.</p>
	<h3>Linux</h3>
	<p>
		Use <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/">Raspberry Pi Imager</a> (version 2.0.11 or newer). Under
		<strong>Operating System</strong> choose <strong>Use custom</strong> and pick the image as downloaded. There's no
		need to extract it first. Then choose your USB drive or microSD card and write.
	</p>
	<h3>Windows</h3>
	<p>
		Use <a href="https://rufus.ie/">Rufus</a> (version 4.7 or newer). Select your USB drive or microSD card, click
		<strong>Select</strong> and pick the image as downloaded, then press <strong>Start</strong>.
		If Rufus asks how to write the image, choose <strong>DD Image mode</strong>. Raspberry Pi Imager for Windows
		works too, the same way as on Linux.
	</p>

	<h2>3. RK3326 handhelds: set up the screen</h2>
	<p>
		Skip this on a PC or Raspberry Pi. Do it before the first boot: after writing, the card shows up on your computer
		as a small drive. What to do depends on your handheld:
	</p>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Odroid Go 2/3/Super, Anbernic RG351M/RG351V, Powkiddy RGB10, GameForce Chi, MagicX XU10:</strong> nothing, go straight to booting.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>R36S and its clones:</strong> their screens differ from unit to unit. Put the handheld's original card
			in your computer, open its <strong>BOOT</strong> partition and copy its <code>.dtb</code> file (for example
			<code>rk3326-r36s-linux.dtb</code>) into the <code>stock</code> folder on the HippOS card. Copy only one file,
			and leave out files ending in <code>-uboot.dtb</code>.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>R33S, EE clone, MagicX XU-Mini M, BatleXP G350 and XiFan models:</strong> open the
			<code>devices</code> folder on the HippOS card, copy your model's file to the top of the card and rename the
			copy to <code>fdtfile.txt</code>. <code>devices/README.txt</code> lists which file is which. If an EE clone's
			screen stays blank, also copy the <code>.dtb</code> as for the R36S.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>4. Boot</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>PC:</strong> turn <strong>Secure Boot off</strong> in your firmware settings; HippOS doesn't boot
			with it on. Plug the drive in, open your machine's boot menu (often <strong>F8</strong>, <strong>F11</strong>
			or <strong>F12</strong> at power-on) and choose the drive.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>PC handheld:</strong> the same, using the handheld's boot menu. On a Steam Deck or ROG Ally, hold
			<strong>Volume Down</strong> and press <strong>Power</strong>.
		</li>
		<li><strong>Raspberry Pi:</strong> put the card or USB drive in and switch it on. The Pi boots from it by itself.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>RK3326 handheld:</strong> put the card in the <strong>first</strong> card slot and switch on. On an
			R36S the screen stays blank on the first boot while HippOS sets it up from the <code>.dtb</code>, then it
			restarts by itself.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		On the first boot HippOS expands its data partition to fill the drive and sets itself up. This takes a little
		longer than usual. Then you land in EmulationStation.
	</p>
	<p>
		If you only see a black screen or a frozen splash on a PC or Pi, try another display output first. On an RK3326
		handheld, check the screen step above, and if it still stays blank ask in <strong>#rk3326</strong> on the
		<a href="/discord">Discord</a> with the name of your handheld. Otherwise, ask in the Discord and include your logs
		(see the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>).
	</p>

	<h2>5. Install to an internal disk (PC, optional)</h2>
	<p>
		HippOS runs fine from USB or microSD. To move it onto an internal drive on a PC or PC handheld, open
		<strong>Main Menu → System Settings → Storage → Install on a new disk</strong>. You can wipe a whole disk,
		install into an existing partition, or use free space next to another OS. Each option needs at least 16 GB.
	</p>

	<h2>6. Add your games</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Over the network:</strong> HippOS shares its data folder over Samba. Open <code>\\HIPPOS\userdata</code> (Windows) or <code>smb://hippos/userdata</code> (Linux/macOS) and copy games into the matching folder under <code>roms</code>.</li>
		<li><strong>External drives:</strong> plug in a drive. HippOS merges its ROMs, BIOS and saves folders into your library automatically.</li>
		<li><strong>Handhelds with two card slots:</strong> leave your games on a card in the second slot. HippOS merges it the same way, including cards set up by other handheld systems: folder names such as <code>genesis</code> or <code>sfc</code> are understood.</li>
		<li><strong>BIOS files:</strong> some systems need BIOS files you supply yourself. Put them in <code>userdata/bios</code>.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Updating</h2>
	<p>
		Installed systems update over the air from <strong>Main Menu → Updates &amp; Downloads</strong>. Updates replace
		the OS only. Your games, saves and settings stay on their own partition. See what changed in the
		<a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.
	</p>

	<p>More detailed guides are in the <a href={DOCS_URL}>HippOS docs</a>, including pages for <a href={`${DOCS_URL}devices/pc-handhelds/`}>PC handhelds</a> and <a href={`${DOCS_URL}devices/rk3326/`}>RK3326 handhelds</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
